body:
### Tested versions

v4.6.1.stable.arch_linux [14d19694e]

### System information

Godot v4.6.1.stable (14d19694e) - Arch Linux

### Issue description

When a C++ declared abstract class is defined in code, and registered to Godot, GDScript does not produce an error when trying to instantiate the class. This does not match the behavior when a GDScript class is defined as abstract, which correctly produces an error.

The editor should notify the user, exactly like the behavior from GDScript defined abstract classes. This is important especially if C++ GDExtension assets are shared with other users unfamiliar with a codebase and its nuances.

When the scene is played, it will produce an error trying to instantiate an object from a nullptr constructor function.

The value of `ClassDB.can_instantiate("MyAbstractClass")` returns false, implying ClassDB has the information to produce an error when a user tries to instantiate the class.

Error Log from IDE:
```
ERROR: Class 'MyAbstractClass' or its base class cannot be instantiated.
   at: _instantiate_internal (core/object/class_db.cpp:576)
   GDScript backtrace (most recent call first):
       [0] _ready (res://demo_abstract.gd:6)
ERROR: Class type: 'MyAbstractClass' is not instantiable.
   at: _new (modules/gdscript/gdscript.cpp:93)
   GDScript backtrace (most recent call first):
       [0] _ready (res://demo_abstract.gd:6)
SCRIPT ERROR: Cannot call method 'get_number' on a null value.
          at: _ready (res://demo_abstract.gd:7)
          GDScript backtrace (most recent call first):
              [0] _ready (res://demo_abstract.gd:7)
```
Error log from GDScript editor:
```
